\par }\pard\plain \s16\ql \li360\ri360\sl-360\slmult0\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\tx9360\tx10080\faauto\adjustright\rin360\lin360\itap0 
\f12\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par Authentication:  In computer systems, methods to ensure that the true user or process is the one using the unique userID assigned.  Methods typically fall into three categories:  something you know, something you have, and something you are.
\par 
\par Authorization:  In computer systems, methods to ensure that a user or process can only access information and perform functions which have been formally approved.  These are the user\rquote s privileges or permissions.
\par 
\par Availability:  The protected information is accessible when and as needed by authorized users and processes.
\par 
\par Confidentiality:  The protected information is accessible to only those people and processes that have been authorized, and all others are prevented from access.
\par 
\par Hacker/cracker:  Someone who attempts to break into a system or network.  \'93White hat\'94 hackers
 consider themselves benign.  They prefer that malicious hackers be called crackers.  Motivations range from intellectual challenge to political and criminal activities.  This book uses the term hacker generically since it is the more widely recognized te
rm.
\par 
\par Identification:  In computer systems, a unique name (or string of characters) given to each user and process.  This is required for tracking activity in a system and relating it to one individual, that is, for auditability and accountability.
\par Information security:  The assurance of confidentiality, integrity, and availability of protected information assets.
\par 
\par Integrity (of information):  The protected information is accurate and reliable, that is, it has not been inappropriately altered or destroyed \endash  either by a person or an electronic process, either inadvertently or intentionally.\line 
\par Risk:  The possibility of an adverse event arising from either (a) threats or (b) vulnerabilities.  
\par 
\par Risk assessment:  The process of analyzing and interpreting risk (NIST
 SP 800-14).  Also called risk analysis.  Risk is comprised of threats and vulnerabilities.  The assessment is a determination of (a) likelihood of occurrence of a particular security event and (b) the severity of its impact.
\par 
\par Risk management:  The processes of risk assessment (identifying and measuring risks) and risk mitigation (reduction or elimination of risks at reasonable expense).  In HIPAA\rquote 
s Security Rule, the term refers to the risk mitigation component, and risk assessment is a separate requirement.
\par 
\par Steganography:  Methods for hiding information.  Usually a technical subject, in this book it is used to refer to simple, non-technical methods for hiding a person\rquote s passwords so they are not written in plain view. 
\par 
\par Threat:  Can be natural, human, or environmental in origin [NIST SP 800-30, p. 13].  Examples include natural disasters such as hurricanes, a malicious attack on a database by a former employee, and a massive power outage.  
\par 
\par Vulnerability:  A flaw or weakness in system security procedures, design, implementation, or internal controls that could be exercised (accidentally triggered or intentionally exploited) and result in a security breach or a violation of the system\rquote 
s security policy. [NIST SP 800-30, p.15]
\par 
